  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nLongwave 500mb troughing currently over the Northeastern U.S \nthis morning continues to break down and progress downstream as \nlow amplitude ridging begins to build across the central U.S. \nThis pattern will support surface high pressure to build across \nthe Great Lakes Region as 850 mb temperatures surge well above \nnormal for early October. As a result of the efficient warm air \nadvection from the south, surface temperatures will approach \nrecord breaking levels, with highs in the 70s for most parts of \nthe CWA this weekend. \n\nConditions turn slightly more active for the rust belt at the start \nof next week as tropical moisture associated with Isaias is expected \nto track northward across the Mississippi Valley before being \ndragged across the lower Great Lakes Region. Most rainfall will \nlikely remain south of the CWA, but areas around Saginaw Bay could \nobserve some fragments of light stratiform rainfall Sunday night. \nThe next chance of widespread rain chances remains towards the end \nof the forecast period as two waves originating from Alberta and the \nAmerican Southwest make their way to the Midwest. Details remain \nblurry on amounts and potential impacts at this time, but moderate \ntroughing will support rainfall to the CWA and temperatures falling \nback into the 50s around the Tuesday and Thursday timeframe.\n   \nForecast Details:  \n\nToday, Saturday and Sunday: Ongoing subsidence aloft will continue \nto build surface high pressure today. Patchy morning fog will begin \nto burn off in the morning hours followed by mostly sunny skies and \nhighs in the 60s for Northern Lower while eastern upper remains in \nthe upper 50s for most areas north of M-28. Light northwest winds \nturn to the southeast overnight into Saturday as aforementioned warm \nair advection commences and pushes low level temperatures well into \nthe teens at 850mb. With efficient mixing to the surface this \nSaturday and Sunday, high temperatures will build well into the 70s \nfor most parts of the CWA. Aforementioned breezy SE flow will create \nslight downloping processes across the Lake Michigan shoreline areas \nof Northern Lower, resulting in temperatures around 80.\n\nMonday through Thursday Outlook: Long-range ensembles depict a small \nwave developing lee of the Canadian Rockies this weekend and making \nits way to the Midwest by Tuesday. Between weak forcing and deep \nsoutherly moisture remaining cut off, QPF amounts remain around a \ngeneral tenth inch or less. More appreciable rain chances remain \nfocused on Thursday's potential precipitation. Deeper troughing \ncurrently over the northern Pacific will deepen over the Southwest \nU.S. this weekend and lift across the northwoods at the end of the \nforecast period. Organization of this low-pressure system appears \norganized with a deep closed midlevel low pressure and an ideal \nNortheastern track of this system. Still too early to forecast \ndetails and potential impacts to the region, but we will continue to \nmonitor this feature as we head into next week.",
